description:
This small project has a library (`shapes.h` and `shapes.cpp`) and a program that uses it (`main.cpp`). The code is finished. What is missing is the file that tells a build system how the pieces fit: `CMakeLists.txt`.

Write it. It should:

1. start with `cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.20)` (or newer) and a `project(...)` line naming the project `Shapes` with `LANGUAGES CXX`;
2. ask for C++20: `set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 20)` and `set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON)`;
3. define a **library** target named `shapes` built from `shapes.cpp`: `add_library(shapes shapes.cpp)`;
4. define an **executable** target named `app` built from `main.cpp`: `add_executable(app main.cpp)`;
5. link the program against the library: `target_link_libraries(app PRIVATE shapes)`.

The check compiles the C++ as usual (the program must still print `12` and `3.14159`) and reads your `CMakeLists.txt` to see that the five pieces are there. On your own machine, with CMake installed, `cmake -S . -B build` followed by `cmake --build build` would then build it anywhere.

starter.main.cpp:
#include <iostream>
#include "shapes.h"

int main()
{
    std::cout << rectangleArea(3, 4) << '\n';
    std::cout << circleArea(1) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

starter.shapes.h:
#pragma once

double rectangleArea(double width, double height);
double circleArea(double radius);

starter.shapes.cpp:
#include "shapes.h"

double rectangleArea(double width, double height)
{
    return width * height;
}

double circleArea(double radius)
{
    return 3.14159 * radius * radius;
}

